          "cvss": "CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:L/UI:R/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H",
          "date": "2026-10-09",
          "description": "The `zip` crate preserves privileged Unix permission bits (SUID, SGID, and sticky bits) from ZIP `external_attributes` during extraction, instead of masking them to safe permission bits. If a privileged process (for example, `root` in an installer, CI/CD pipeline, or container build) extracts an attacker-controlled ZIP archive using `ZipArchive::extract()` or the stream extraction API, files can be created as setuid-root or setgid. This behavior allows the creation of SUID/SGID files during extraction, which can lead to local privilege escalation depending on the execution environment.\n\nFor further details see: https://github.com/zip-rs/zip2/security/advisories/GHSA-vv5m-jxxh-4627",
          "expect-deleted": false,
          "id": "RUSTSEC-2026-0367",
          "informational": null,
          "keywords": [
            "privilege-escalation",
            "suid",
            "sgid"
          ],
          "license": "CC0-1.0",
          "package": "zip",
          "references": [],
          "related": [],
          "source": null,
          "title": " Insecure Preservation of SUID/SGID Bits during ZIP Extraction Leading to Potential Privilege Escalation",
          "url": null,
